@meta
id: 16-06
title: Why vim refused
chapter: 16
type: lesson
skills: buffer-switch, buffer-list
par: 125
minutes: 6
vscode: the unsaved dot on the tab, and nothing stops you clicking away
open: order.entity.ts order.repository.ts order.provider.ts
vimrc: set nohidden

@brief
Every hop so far has been preceded by `:w`, and you were told the
reason would come. Here it is. Out of the box, vim will not let a
window switch away from a buffer with unsaved changes if that buffer is
not visible anywhere else. Make an edit, type `:bn`, and you get

    E37: No write since last change (add ! to override)

The buffer has not been lost; vim has just declined to hide it. There
are three ways through. Write first, which is what you have been doing.
Add `!` to the command, `:bn!`, which hides the buffer with its changes
intact rather than discarding them. Or set the `hidden` option, which
makes every buffer hideable and turns the refusal off for good. The
starter vimrc this dojo ships sets `hidden`, so you may never have met
E37; this lesson turns it off so that you do.

Three edits, one per file, and this time do not write until the very
end. Let vim refuse once so you have seen it, then get past it.

  1. `order.entity.ts`: `currency: string;` directly under `total`.
  2. `order.repository.ts`: `currency: record.currency,` under `total`
     in `toEntity`.
  3. `order.provider.ts`: the refund call becomes
     `this.paymentService.refund(order.id, order.currency)`.

Then look at `:ls` before you finish. A hidden buffer with unsaved
changes shows `h` and `+` where the current one shows `%a`. Write them
all with `:wa` and leave with `:qa`.

@keys
| `:bn!` | next buffer, hiding this one even though it is modified |
| `:b! name` | the same for a named hop |
| `:set hidden` | stop asking; in a vimrc, `set hidden` |
| `:wa` | write every modified buffer, hidden or not |
| `:qa` | quit every window; refuses if anything is still unsaved |
| `:qa!` | quit and throw away unsaved changes in every buffer |

@hints
Make the entity edit, then `:bn` and read the message. Then `:bn!`.
--
`h +` in `:ls` is a buffer you cannot see with changes you have not
written. It is the reason `:qa` will refuse until you `:wa`.
--
`:wa` then `:qa`, or `:wqa` which is both. Either way, nothing hidden
is lost.

@solution
    /total<Enter>  o currency: string; <Esc>  :bn!<Enter>
    /total<Enter>  o currency: record.currency, <Esc>  :bn!<Enter>
    /refund(<Enter>  f)  i , order.currency <Esc>  :wa<Enter>  :qa<Enter>

About a hundred and ten keystrokes with the two forced hops; four
more if you `:bn` first and are refused, which is worth doing once.

`hidden` is the setting almost everyone turns on and then forgets they
turned on. With it, buffers behave like VS Code tabs: you can wander
between files with unsaved changes in several of them, and `:ls` keeps
the score with `+`. What you give up is a guard rail. Vim still refuses
to exit while a modified buffer exists, but `:qa` is now the only
thing standing between you and forgetting a file, and it will tell you
which one.

`:wqa` is the safe exit either way. `:qa!` is the one to think about
before pressing Enter: it discards every unwritten change in every
buffer, including the ones you cannot see.

@start order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
  cancellationReason?: string;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'SHIPPED' | 'CANCELLED';

@expected order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
  currency: string;
  cancellationReason?: string;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'SHIPPED' | 'CANCELLED';

@start order.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Order as OrderRecord } from '@prisma/client';
import { BaseRepository } from '../../infrastructure/base.repository';
import { Order } from './order.entity';

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository extends BaseRepository<OrderRecord, Order> {
  public toEntity(record: OrderRecord): Order {
    return {
      id: record.id,
      customerId: record.customerId,
      status: record.status,
      total: record.total,
      cancellationReason: record.cancellationReason ?? undefined,
    };
  }
}

@expected order.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Order as OrderRecord } from '@prisma/client';
import { BaseRepository } from '../../infrastructure/base.repository';
import { Order } from './order.entity';

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository extends BaseRepository<OrderRecord, Order> {
  public toEntity(record: OrderRecord): Order {
    return {
      id: record.id,
      customerId: record.customerId,
      status: record.status,
      total: record.total,
      currency: record.currency,
      cancellationReason: record.cancellationReason ?? undefined,
    };
  }
}

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id, order.currency);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}
